<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Sigismund, Elector of Brandenburg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1572">1572</a>–<a href="/years/1619">1619</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">House of Hohenzollern</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Sigismund was Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> from 1608 to 1619. Through his marriage to Anna of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> he acquired claims to the Jülich–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kleve">Cleves</a> inheritance, gaining Cleves, Mark and Ravensberg by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-xanten">Treaty of Xanten</a> in 1614, and in 1618 he inherited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia</a>. His conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvinism">Calvinism</a> in 1613 left a Reformed dynasty ruling a Lutheran people.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-sigismund-elector-of-brandenburg.jpg" alt="John Sigismund, Elector of Brandenburg" width="320" height="384" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous / Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johann_Sigismund_Grunewald.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John Sigismund was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halle-saale">Halle an der Saale</a>, the son of Joachim III Frederick, Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, and his first wife Catherine of Brandenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kostrzyn-nad-odra">Küstrin</a>. On 30 October 1594 he married Anna of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, the eldest daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-frederick-duke-of-prussia">Albert Frederick, Duke of Prussia</a>, who had no sons. She was the elder sister of his stepmother. The marriage made possible the union of Brandenburg and Prussia, and through his wife he became a major claimant in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-julich-succession">War of the Jülich Succession</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He succeeded his father as margrave and elector in 1608. In 1611 he travelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, where on 16 November he did feudal homage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-iii-vasa">Sigismund III Vasa</a>, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia</a> then was. For several years before 1618 he acted as regent for the mentally disturbed Albert Frederick, and when the duke died he officially became Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-xanten">Treaty of Xanten</a> of 1614 he received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kleve">Cleves</a>, Mark and Ravensberg. A stroke in 1616 left him without full recovery, and he died in 1619.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>His most significant act was his move from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvinism">Calvinism</a>. He had earlier equalised the rights of Catholics and Protestants in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> under pressure from the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. He probably became a Calvinist during a visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> in 1606, but he took communion publicly in the Calvinist rite only on 25 December 1613. Most of his subjects, and his wife Anna, stayed deeply Lutheran.</p><p>In February 1614 the elector and his Calvinist officials drew up plans for a mass conversion of the population, under the rule cuius regio, eius religio, and serious protests followed. In 1615 he gave up all attempts at forcible conversion and allowed his subjects to be Lutheran or Calvinist as conscience dictated, so that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-prussia">Brandenburg-Prussia</a> became a bi-confessional state. His son George William succeeded him, and his daughter Maria Eleonora married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and was the mother of Christina of Sweden.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John Sigismund, Elector of Brandenburg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1618 Elector <b>John Sigismund</b> inherited the Duchy of Prussia, the secularised territory of the Teutonic Order around Königsberg, which was held as a fief of the Polish crown.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Joachim II introduced the Lutheran Reformation in 1539, taking communion in both kinds at Spandau, and the elector <b>John Sigismund</b> became a Calvinist in 1613, leaving a Reformed dynasty ruling a Lutheran people.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-brandenburg">Margraviate of Brandenburg · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halle-saale">Halle (Saale)</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.48,11.97&amp;year=1602" aria-label="Halle (Saale) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4821 381 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of John Sigismund, Elector of Brandenburg"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5271 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5271 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.48,11.97&amp;year=1602">What was here in 1602? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
